  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 237 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nSynoptically, for the long term, not much has changed. The ridge off \nthe west coast should begin to strengthen and move slowly to the \neast. Guidance suggests that by Friday, the ridge begins to stall \nand remain nearly stationary over the western US for the rest of the \nweekend. During this period, our upper level flow will start off as \nzonal and begin to transition to come from the north-northwest as \nthe ridge moves. \n\nOverall, it looks to be hot and dry Thursday through Sunday. \nStarting Thursday, temperatures begin to warm up to the high 80s to \nlow 90s. Thursday has the best precipitation chances, around \n10-20%, as a shortwave moves through the region. Friday though \nSunday, high temperatures are forecast to be in high 90s to low \n100s. Saturday and Sunday have the greatest chance of reaching \ntriple digits for the majority of the CWA. For Saturday and \nSunday afternoon, relative humidity values are forecast to be \nin mid-teens for areas west of KS Hwy 27. Wind Gusts are \nforecast to be around or less than 25 mph. GFDI values are in \nthe range of 20-40 which would promote high to very high fire \ngrowth. While the winds do not met fire weather criteria, and \nthis is around a week out, it is something to keep and eye on \ngiven the hot temperatures and the drier fuels in the area.",
